responsive-tracks-sketch-0.bodyMarkdown:
Responsive CSS avoids assuming one fixed screen size. Percentages, `rem`, and fractional Grid units can size content relative to its container, root font size, or available track space.

**Worked example:**

```css
main {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 72rem;
}
```

**Line by line:** `main {` targets the existing semantic main region. `width: 100%;` lets it use the available width of its containing block. `max-width: 72rem;` prevents that fluid width from growing without bound. The closing `}` ends the rule.

responsive-tracks-sketch-1.bodyMarkdown:
`minmax(min, max)` lets a Grid track stay above a useful minimum while still growing when room is available.

**Worked example:**

```css
main {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
}
```

**Line by line:** `main {` continues styling the existing Grid container. `grid-template-columns:` defines its column tracks. `repeat(auto-fit, ...)` lets the browser create as many tracks as will fit. `minmax(16rem, 1fr)` keeps each track at least 16rem wide while allowing it to grow into available fractional space. The closing `}` completes the rule.

responsive-tracks-sketch-2.bodyMarkdown:
Fluid tracks can reduce unnecessary breakpoints because the Grid itself adapts to available space before a media query is needed.

**Worked example:**

```css
main {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  margin-inline: auto;
}
```

**Line by line:** `main {` targets the same Grid container. `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));` creates fluid tracks that automatically fit the available width. `margin-inline: auto;` centers the container horizontally when its maximum width leaves extra inline space. The closing `}` ends the rule.
